          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also called hypertension, means the pressure in your arteries stays raised over time, not just on one occasion. Blood pressure is written as two numbers. The top number (systolic) is the pressure when the heart beats. The bottom number (diastolic) is the pressure between beats. Both are measured in millimeters of mercury. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nAdult readings taken in a clinic are classified like this, according to US society guidance (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*):\n\n- **Normal:** top number under 120 and bottom number under 80.\n- **Elevated:** top number 120 to 129, bottom number under 80.\n- **Stage 1 hypertension:** top number 130 to 139, or bottom number 80 to 89.\n- **Stage 2 hypertension:** top number 140 or more, or bottom number 90 or more.\n\nIf the two numbers fall into different categories, the higher category applies.\n\n**Why one high reading is not a diagnosis:** Blood pressure changes through the day. It moves with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep and time of day. A single high reading, especially in a doctor's office, can reflect these factors or the stress of the visit. Guidance says a diagnosis requires an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions. Home readings or a 24-hour monitor can also show \"white-coat\" hypertension (high only in the clinic) or \"masked\" hypertension (normal in the clinic but raised at other times). (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n\nHypertension usually causes no symptoms, so it is often found during routine checks rather than because someone feels unwell.\n\nIf you have had a high reading, the next step is usually repeat measurements, not a conclusion. A clinician can tell you how to take readings at home and what they mean for you. I can't tell you whether you have hypertension. If you have chest pain, severe headache, shortness of breath, weakness, or confusion, seek emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
